#opener("The PiForma wears a familiar face, but Raspberry Pi OS does the work underneath. This chapter explains the everyday business of pointing, typing, opening programs and keeping what you make. You need not learn the wiring to save a file.")
#heading(level:2,"Finding your way around the desktop")
#para("The desktop is the working screen behind your application windows. PiForma Panel supplies the menu bar at the top; AtEase is the folder-like launcher for programs. The Control Strip provides another way to open or return to programs.")
#para("Figure 1-7 in Chapter 1 shows these parts together. The launcher has At Ease Items and sites tabs; Clippy occupies the lower-right corner. The clock appears at the upper right of the system bar.")
#para("Choose a menu by clicking its name, then click the command you want. An unavailable command may be dimmed. Read a dialog before answering: it may ask where to save a file, whether to discard changes, or whether to stop the computer.")
#para("The Macintosh appearance does not make every classic Macintosh command applicable. Use the commands shown by the running program. Its menus and the desktop's top menu bar can have different jobs.")
#heading(level:2,"Using the mouse")
#para("Move the mouse across its pad to move the pointer. If you reach the edge, lift the mouse, set it down nearer the center and continue. The mouse need not point at the screen; the little eye underneath follows the surface below it.")
#para("Click means press and release the left button once. A click selects an item or operates a button. Double-click means two quick clicks without moving the pointer between them; many file views use it to open an item. AtEase program buttons need only one click. Repeated clicking while a program starts can produce more windows than you intended.")
#para("To drag, point at the object, hold the left button, move the mouse, then release. Drag a window by its title bar, rather than by its contents. Dragging a file may move or copy it, so practice with windows before rearranging your work.")
#para("Roll the wheel to scroll through a longer page or list. Point over the area you want to scroll. Scroll bars provide another way to reach material outside a window's visible area.")
#para("The right button may show a menu of actions for the item under the pointer. Its behavior depends on the program. This is the mysterious “second left click” in the illustration; it is a real right button, despite our best efforts. The Sync button is unnecessary for the normal one-receiver setup.")
#heading(level:2,"Using the keyboard")
#para("Switch the keyboard on at its upper-right edge. It uses Bluetooth, independently of the mouse's USB receiver. First click in the window or text field where you want to type. The insertion point marks where new text will go; moving the mouse pointer alone does not move it.")
#para("Use the letter, number and punctuation keys to enter text. Hold Shift while typing a letter for a capital or a key's shifted symbol. Return commonly ends a paragraph in a document; in a dialog it may accept the current choice, so read the dialog first. Use the arrow keys to move through text where the program supports them.")
#para("To replace text, select it by dragging across it, then type the replacement. Deleting selected text removes the whole selection. If you remove something by mistake, look for Undo in that application's Edit menu before doing more work.")
#para("Use an application's own Cut, Copy and Paste commands when available. Copy leaves the original selection in place; Cut removes it; Paste inserts the copied or cut material at the insertion point. Check the selection before cutting, and the insertion point before pasting.")
#para("The vintage key legends do not establish a modern shortcut map. Follow commands displayed by the program instead of assuming that an Apple-labeled key performs a particular action. Pairing and special key combinations are not covered here. If ordinary typing fails, use the keyboard checks in Appendix B.")
#pagebreak(weak:true)
#heading(level:2,"Opening programs with AtEase")
#annotated("AtEase",none,[
#para("AtEase has two folder tabs. At Ease Items holds the programs; sites holds a selection of websites the builder finds interesting, a collection still being tidied. Each tab shows only what fits in its window; there is no second page yet. Click a tab to switch between them, then click a button once. Allow time for the program to open. A launcher button starts a program; it is not the document you may later create inside it.")
#launcher-detail()
